{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132558",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time needed to serve the next person. The time needed to serve the next person can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since one person is served every $4$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next person will be served at least $2$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/4}{|}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-2/4} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-132558\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>Since the waiting time for one customer to be served at the cafeteria varies continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until serving the next person as an exponential distribution.</p>\n<p>An exponential random variable <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"35\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D44B TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> with rate <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"36\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D706 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>λ</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> has the following probability density function:</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" display=\"true\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"37\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math display=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"margin-left: 0px; margin-right: 0px;\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D453 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mrow spa